If you are going to do this as a new effort in
today's software writing "best practice" model, you
really, really, really want to write this portable
bones file in what has become the software industry
standard for portable files across pretty much all
applications types: XML.

The pain that it has caused the software industry
over the last half century to use anything but
self-describing file formats has grown overwhelming.

It is now recognized that the effort to convert the
data of any dataset intended for transmittal between
hetrogenous computing environments, or meant for
long term storage, to a standard, plain ASCII,
admittedly bulky, self-describing format is so fully
repaid, any other choice is a foolish waste of
effort.

XML has becoming overwhelmingly what that set of
motherhood issues descriptors is understood to
imply today.

In the case of bones file transmittals, having the
transmittal format be self-describing, and free of
problems like endianness, will make it easy for
specialists in each computing environment to assure
the the format and bones file written in it imports
and exports smoothly.

No, I will _not_ enter into a debate about this,
here, this isn't a programming issues newsgroup.

I've been part of that industry for 45 of those 50
years, and I've seen the data lossage from use of
ideosyncratic data transmission formats too many
times to count.

The issue is closed, for me, and I'm busily teaching
myself XML, which I neglected, to my current regret,
to learn when it first appeared, so that in the
future I can take my own advice.

HTH

